1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:09,226
The brush clears a tall column, and its surface gleams like glass. It is made of one piece of sandstone, polished until it shines.

2
00:00:09,226 --> 00:00:15,141
This shine is the signature of Mauryan stonework. How do you make stone shine?

3
00:00:33,183 --> 00:00:40,341
How do we know? We have the monuments themselves. Archaeologists dug up the pillared hall at Kumrahar.

4
00:00:40,341 --> 00:00:44,743
Inscriptions on the pillars tell us who raised them, and why.

5
00:01:01,283 --> 00:01:09,109
Mauryan art has two streams. One is court art, made by royal order. The other is the art of the people.

6
00:01:09,109 --> 00:01:11,787
The great pillars are the best-known court art.

7
00:01:11,787 --> 00:01:18,099
Each is cut from a single piece of sandstone, quarried at Chunar, and polished to a high gloss.

8
00:01:18,099 --> 00:01:26,978
Each carries an animal capital, such as a bull, an elephant or a lion. The capital stands on an abacus, above a bell-shaped base.

9
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:32,000
The Sarnath lion capital, which is our national emblem, is the best known.

10
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,692
At Kumrahar, near Patna, archaeologists found the remains of a great pillared hall, thought to have had eighty pillars.

11
00:01:38,692 --> 00:01:46,681
At the Barabar hills, caves were cut into hard granite and polished inside. The Lomas Rishi cave has a carved front.

12
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:56,886
The art of the people includes terracotta figures, and large stone figures called yakshas and yakshis, such as the polished yakshi from Didarganj, whose date is still debated.

13
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:18,335
Scholars disagree. Where did Mauryan court art get its inspiration?

14
00:02:18,335 --> 00:02:26,576
One view points to Persian influence, such as the bell-shaped capital and the polish, because Persian craftsmen were at work in the north-west.

15
00:02:26,576 --> 00:02:32,930
Another view points to a native tradition, which had earlier worked in wood, and moved to stone.

16
00:02:49,483 --> 00:02:55,971
Carve this tip into memory. Chunar stone, mirror polish. animal capital.

17
00:02:55,971 --> 00:03:01,633
Three things mark a Mauryan pillar: sandstone, polish and an animal on top.

18
00:03:18,183 --> 00:03:33,483
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. From which stone are Mauryan pillars carved? The answer: sandstone from Chunar.

19
00:03:33,483 --> 00:03:46,138
Number two. Which hills have polished rock-cut caves? The answer: the Barabar hills. Number three.

20
00:03:46,138 --> 00:03:58,006
Name the baked clay used for popular art. The answer: terracotta. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:03:58,006 --> 00:04:02,698
Describe the main features of Mauryan art and architecture.

22
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:30,809
The pillar goes into its case. Its polished surface still catches the light. Three facts to take away.

23
00:04:30,809 --> 00:04:41,299
One piece of Chunar sandstone. Mirror-like Mauryan polish. Topped by an animal capital. Next gallery: After the Mauryas.

